            meta: "5 min · interactive", demo: .color,
            who: ["Color vision deficiency — about 1 in 12 men", "Low vision", "Monochrome displays", "Screen glare"],
            takeaways: ["Pair every color cue with a second one: text, an icon, a pattern, a shape or position.", "Links inside body text need an underline — or 3:1 contrast with surrounding text plus a non-color cue on hover and focus.", "In charts, label lines and areas directly instead of relying on a color legend.", "Errors and required fields need words, not just red."],
            failures: ["Form errors shown only by a red border", "Red and green status dots with no label", "Line charts where series differ only by hue"],
            check: ["View the screen in grayscale. Is anything lost?", "Run it through a color-blindness simulator.", "For each color you use, ask: what else tells people this?"],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Required fields are marked only by red labels. Which fix satisfies 1.4.1?", options: ["Make the red darker", "Add “(required)” or an asterisk with a legend", "Switch to orange"], answer: 1, why: "Changing the color doesn’t help — the cue can’t depend on color at all. Text or a symbol does."),
            spec: ["Beside each status color, write its second cue: icon, label or pattern.", "Specify links inside body text as underlined by default.", "In charts, note the direct label and the marker or pattern for each series."],
            review: ["If this screen were printed in gray, what would be lost?", "Which states differ only by hue?", "Does the chart still read without its legend colors?"],
            tradeoff: "Extra icons and labels add noise. The answer is a quieter second cue, such as weight, position or a small glyph. It is not removing the cue.",
            system: "Build the second cue into status components (badge, alert, field error) so nobody can ship color alone.",
            pitch: "About 1 in 12 men has a color vision deficiency. A color-only status is invisible to someone in almost every team that uses your product."
        ),
        "1.4.10": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Low vision users who zoom", "Screen magnifier users", "People on small screens", "Older adults"],
            takeaways: ["At 320 CSS px wide, content fits without scrolling in two directions. That equals a 1280px-wide window zoomed to 400%.", "For content that scrolls sideways, such as vertical text, the test is 256 CSS px tall.", "No content or function is lost. The narrow layout can stack columns, collapse navigation into a menu and wrap text.", "Exception: parts that need a two-dimensional layout, such as maps, data tables, diagrams, video and editors whose toolbars must stay in view. They may scroll; the rest of the page still reflows."],
            failures: ["A fixed-width layout that scrolls sideways at 400% zoom", "Sticky headers and footers that fill the screen when zoomed", "Content hidden in the mobile layout with no way to reach it"],
            check: ["Set a frame to 320px wide and lay out every key screen in it.", "In the build, set the window to 1280px and zoom to 400%. Read the page scrolling only down.", "Check that nothing is cut off, overlapping or missing compared with the wide view."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "At 320px a data table is wider than the screen. Everything else on the page fits. Does the page pass?", options: ["No. Nothing may scroll sideways", "Only if the table turns into cards", "Yes. The table may scroll sideways on its own"], answer: 2, why: "A data table needs rows and columns to make sense, so it is exempt. The rest of the page must reflow, so give the table its own scroll area."),
            spec: ["Provide a 320px-wide layout for each template, and say what changes.", "Mark which regions may scroll in two directions, and why.", "Say how sticky elements behave when space is tight, such as becoming static."],
            review: ["What does this look like at 400% zoom?", "Which parts truly need two-dimensional scrolling?", "How much of the screen is left once sticky bars are shown?"],
            tradeoff: "Hiding things on small screens keeps them tidy, but anything hidden must still be reachable. Collapse into menus and disclosures rather than removing content. Text size alone is 1.4.4.",
            system: "Make every component responsive down to 320px, and give tables and media a built-in scroll container.",
            pitch: "Zooming in is how many people with low vision read. If they have to scroll sideways on every line, they give up."
        ),
        "1.4.11": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Low vision", "Color vision deficiency", "Aging eyes", "Screen glare"],
            takeaways: ["Anything you must see to use a control needs 3:1 against what is next to it: field borders, icons, toggles, checkboxes.", "States count. Focus rings and selected states need 3:1 too.", "In charts and diagrams, the parts needed to understand the graphic need 3:1.", "Inactive controls and purely decorative graphics are exempt."],
            failures: ["Pale gray input borders on white", "Light icon-only buttons", "A toggle whose on and off states differ by a faint tint"],
            check: ["Sample each control edge and icon against its background.", "Check every state: default, hover, focus, selected.", "In charts, check neighboring segments against each other."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A text field has a #CCCCCC border on white, about 1.6:1. Nothing else marks its edges. Does it pass?", options: ["Yes. Borders are decorative", "No. It needs 3:1", "No. It needs 4.5:1"], answer: 1, why: "The border is the only thing showing where the field is, so it needs 3:1. The 4.5:1 rule is for text."),
            spec: ["Note the ratio beside each border, icon and state indicator.", "Specify focus and selected states with their own colors.", "For charts, list the palette with neighboring-color ratios, or add separators."],
            review: ["Could you find every input on this screen with your eyes half closed?", "Which icons carry meaning with no label?", "Do selected and unselected look different without color?"],
            tradeoff: "Quiet, barely-there borders are fashionable and they fail. Keep the calm look with a darker hairline that reaches 3:1.",
            system: "Define border and icon tokens that reach 3:1 on every surface they are allowed on.",
            pitch: "If people cannot see where a field is, they cannot fill it in. That is a conversion problem before it is a compliance one."
        ),
        "1.4.12": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Dyslexia", "Low vision", "Cognitive disabilities", "People using reading extensions"],
            takeaways: ["When people set line height to 1.5× the font size, paragraph spacing to 2×, letter spacing to 0.12× and word spacing to 0.16×, no content or function is lost.", "You do not have to design with these values. Your layout has to survive people applying them.", "Wider spacing makes text longer and taller. Containers that grow with their text survive; fixed boxes clip.", "It applies to real text in the page, not text in images. Languages that do not use one of these properties only need the ones they use."],
            failures: ["Fixed-height cards that cut off the last line", "Button labels that overflow or get truncated", "Text that overlaps the element below it"],
            check: ["Apply a text spacing bookmarklet to the build and use the page.", "In your file, make a test version of key components with the four values applied.", "Look for clipped, overlapping or truncated text, especially in buttons, tabs and badges."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Brand guidelines set body line height to 1.2. Does that fail 1.4.12?", options: ["No, as long as the layout survives people raising it", "Yes. Line height must be at least 1.5", "Yes, unless it is only used for headings"], answer: 0, why: "This criterion is about what happens when people override spacing, not about your defaults. Generous spacing still helps readability, but here the test is whether anything breaks."),
            spec: ["Mark containers that hold text as hugging their content, not fixed height.", "Say how labels behave when they get longer: wrap, grow or scroll, never clip.", "Avoid line height and spacing values that rely on text staying exactly one line."],
            review: ["Which elements have a fixed height with text inside?", "What happens to these tabs if every word gets wider?", "Where are we truncating with an ellipsis, and can people still read the full text?"],
            tradeoff: "Tight, fixed boxes give a precise look and break first. Let containers grow, and set minimum heights rather than fixed ones, so the design keeps its shape at default and survives at wider spacing.",
            system: "Build text containers with auto layout and minimum heights, and test each component with the four spacing values before release.",
            pitch: "Many people with dyslexia read better with more space between letters and lines. If our layout breaks when they add it, we have locked them out."
        ),
        "1.4.13": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Screen magnifier users", "Low vision", "Tremors and limited dexterity", "Cognitive disabilities"],
            takeaways: ["Content that appears on hover or keyboard focus, such as tooltips, popovers and hover menus, must be dismissible, hoverable and persistent.", "Dismissible: people can close it without moving the pointer or focus, usually with Esc. Hoverable: if hover opened it, the pointer can move onto it without it vanishing.", "Persistent: it stays until hover or focus moves away, the person dismisses it, or its information is no longer valid. No timeouts.", "Exception: content the browser controls, like the native title tooltip. Content opened by a click is not covered."],
            failures: ["A tooltip that disappears when the pointer moves toward it", "A hover card that covers the next field and cannot be closed", "A popover that fades out after a few seconds"],
            check: ["Hover each trigger, then move the pointer onto the content. Does it stay?", "Focus each trigger with the keyboard and press Esc. Does the content close and focus stay put?", "Wait. Does anything disappear on its own?"],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A help tooltip appears on hover and covers the field below it. What must it support?", options: ["Closing by itself after 3 seconds", "A close button inside the tooltip, and nothing else", "Esc to close it, and staying open while the pointer moves onto it"], answer: 2, why: "A timeout breaks persistence, and a close button means moving the pointer, which may close it anyway. Esc closes it in place, and hoverability lets magnifier users read it."),
            spec: ["For each hover or focus reveal, specify the trigger, how it closes and that Esc dismisses it.", "Show the hover bridge: no gap between trigger and content that closes it.", "State that it has no timeout."],
            review: ["Can someone zoomed in to 400% move onto this tooltip and read it?", "How do I get rid of this without moving my mouse?", "Should this be on click instead of hover?"],
            tradeoff: "Hover reveals keep screens clean, but they are fragile. Keep essential information visible, and use hover and focus only for extras that follow these three rules.",
            system: "Build one tooltip and one popover component with Esc dismissal, hoverability and no timeout, and ban custom hover reveals outside them.",
            pitch: "People who magnify the screen see one small piece at a time. A tooltip that vanishes when they move toward it is help they can never read."
        ),
        "1.4.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "6 min · interactive", demo: .contrast,
            who: ["Low vision", "Color vision deficiency", "Aging eyes", "Anyone in bright sunlight", "Cheap or dim screens"],
            takeaways: ["Body text needs 4.5:1. Large text — 24px regular, or about 18.7px bold — needs 3:1.", "Measure against the real background. Over gradients and photos, test the lightest spot behind the text.", "Never round up. 4.49:1 fails.", "Logos, purely decorative text and inactive (disabled) controls are exempt."],
            failures: ["Light gray helper, placeholder and caption text", "White text on light brand colors — sky blue, yellow, orange", "Text over photos with no overlay or scrim"],
            check: ["Sample text and background with a contrast checker such as WebAIM or Stark.", "Check every state: hover, pressed, selected, error.", "For text on images, test the lightest area behind it."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A 16px regular label in #949494 sits on white — 3.03:1. Does it pass?", options: ["Yes — it’s above 3:1", "No — body text needs 4.5:1", "Only at Level A"], answer: 1, why: "16px regular isn’t “large text”, so 4.5:1 applies. #767676 is the lightest gray that passes on white — worth memorizing."),
            spec: ["Write the color pair and its measured ratio next to each text style.", "Where text sits on an image, note the scrim or overlay it depends on.", "List every state with its own pair: hover, pressed, selected, error."],
            review: ["Which pairs here were measured, and which were judged by eye?", "What happens to this text in dark mode and in the error state?", "Does anything look disabled that is actually active?"],
            tradeoff: "The brand color fails on white? Keep the hue for large type, fills and graphics, and choose a darker step for body text. An exception for one team becomes the standard for all.",
            system: "Ship color tokens as approved pairs, not loose swatches. If a pair is not in the system, nobody has checked it.",
            pitch: "Low-contrast text is one of the most common failures audits find, and the cheapest to fix once, in tokens."
        ),
        "1.4.4": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Low vision", "Older adults", "People on small screens", "Anyone without their glasses"],
            takeaways: ["Text can be enlarged to 200% without losing content or function.", "Design for growth: containers expand, text wraps, nothing is clipped.", "Avoid fixed heights on anything that holds text.", "On phones, respect the system text size setting."],
            failures: ["Text cut off or overlapping when enlarged", "Fixed-height buttons that clip two-line labels", "Layouts that only work with one line of text"],
            check: ["Zoom the browser to 200% and use the page.", "Set your phone to its largest text size and open the app.", "Stress-test components with double-length text."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "At 200% text size a tab label is cut off with an ellipsis, and there is no other way to read it. Does it pass?", options: ["Yes. Truncation is a standard pattern", "No. Content is lost", "Yes, at Level AA"], answer: 1, why: "If the full label cannot be read, content is lost. Let it wrap, or let the tabs scroll."),
            spec: ["Show how each component grows: wrap, expand or scroll.", "Mark containers as hugging their content, not fixed height.", "Include a large-text version of key screens."],
            review: ["What does this screen look like at the largest text size?", "Which elements have fixed heights?", "Where will long translations break this layout?"],
            tradeoff: "Tight, pixel-perfect layouts break first. Flexible layouts look a little less controlled and survive real use, including translation.",
            system: "Build components with auto layout and test each one at 200% before release.",
            pitch: "Many people run their phone at a larger text size. A layout that breaks under it breaks for them every day."
        ),
        "2.1.1": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Keyboard users", "Screen reader users", "Switch and voice control users", "People with tremors"],
            takeaways: ["Everything people can do with a mouse or touch, they can also do with a keyboard alone, without needing to press keys at a certain speed.", "Many assistive tools, such as switches and voice control, work through the keyboard interface. If the keyboard cannot do it, they cannot either.", "Developers build it, but designers decide whether a keyboard path exists: hover-only menus, drag-only lists and custom widgets need one designed.", "The only exception is when the result depends on the path of movement, such as freehand drawing. Dragging to reorder is not exempt."],
            failures: ["Actions that only appear on hover", "Drag-and-drop with no other way to move items", "Custom dropdowns, sliders or date pickers with no keyboard behavior"],
            check: ["Put the mouse away and complete every key task with Tab, Enter, Space, Esc and arrow keys.", "For each hover or drag interaction, find the keyboard way to do the same thing.", "Check custom components against the standard keyboard pattern for that type of widget."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "In a table, the Edit and Delete actions appear only when the mouse hovers over a row. What do you specify?", options: ["Nothing. Most people use a mouse here", "Show them on keyboard focus too, or keep a visible “More” button on each row", "A hint above the table: “Hover over a row to see actions”"], answer: 1, why: "Keyboard users never hover, so for them the actions do not exist. Revealing them on focus, or a button that is always there, gives everyone the same path."),
            spec: ["For every custom component, write the keyboard behavior: which keys do what.", "For each hover or drag action, show the keyboard alternative in the frames.", "Mark which elements are reachable with Tab and which use arrow keys inside a group."],
            review: ["How would you do this without a mouse?", "What is only visible on hover here?", "Is this a standard control, or are we inventing keyboard behavior?"],
            tradeoff: "Hiding actions until hover keeps cards clean, but leaves keyboard users out. Show the actions on focus as well as hover, or keep one visible “More” button that opens them all.",
            system: "Document the keyboard behavior in every component’s spec, next to its states, and prefer standard controls over custom ones.",
            pitch: "Keyboard access is the base layer that screen readers, switches and voice control all depend on. Without it, the feature does not exist for them."
        ),
        "2.2.1": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and reading disabilities", "Screen reader users", "Motor disabilities", "People working in a second language"],
            takeaways: ["If you set a time limit, people can turn it off, adjust it to at least ten times the default, or extend it.", "To extend, people are warned before time runs out, get at least 20 seconds to extend with a simple action such as one button, and can do it at least ten times.", "Some people need far longer to read, type or move through a screen. A limit that feels generous to you can lock them out.", "Exceptions: real-time events like auctions, limits that are essential to the activity, and limits longer than 20 hours."],
            failures: ["A session that expires with no warning, losing the form", "A timed checkout with no way to ask for more time", "A toast with an Undo button that vanishes before people can reach it"],
            check: ["List every timer in the flow, visible or not: sessions, holds, toasts, auto-advancing steps.", "For each one, find how people turn it off, adjust it or extend it.", "Check the warning gives at least 20 seconds and that extending is a single, simple action."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A banking app signs people out after 10 minutes of inactivity. What is the simplest way to meet 2.2.1?", options: ["Show a countdown in the header so people can see it", "Warn before time runs out, with a “Stay signed in” button and at least 20 seconds to press it", "Make the limit 15 minutes instead"], answer: 1, why: "A visible countdown still ends with no choice, and a longer fixed limit is still a fixed limit. A warning with a one-press extend lets anyone take the time they need."),
            spec: ["List each time limit, its length, and whether it can be turned off, adjusted or extended.", "Design the warning dialog: when it appears, its wording, and the extend button.", "Note what happens when time does run out, and what data is kept."],
            review: ["Where in this flow can time run out on someone?", "Who decided this limit, and what is it protecting?", "If someone is twice as slow as you, what happens?"],
            tradeoff: "Security and stock teams want short limits. Keep the limit, but always warn and let people extend it with one action. That keeps the protection without punishing slow users.",
            system: "Provide one timeout warning pattern in the library, with the warning time and extend action built in.",
            pitch: "Every surprise timeout is a lost form and a frustrated customer. A warning with one button keeps them, and keeps the security."
        ),
        "2.2.2": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Attention-related disabilities", "Cognitive and reading disabilities", "Screen reader and magnifier users", "Anyone reading slowly"],
            takeaways: ["Anything that moves, blinks or scrolls on its own for more than 5 seconds, next to other content, needs a way to pause, stop or hide it.", "Content that updates on its own, such as a live feed or ticker, needs a way to pause, stop or hide it, or to control how often it updates.", "Movement pulls the eye away from what people are trying to read. For some people it makes the rest of the page unusable.", "Motion that is essential to the activity is exempt. A decorative or promotional animation almost never is."],
            failures: ["An auto-advancing carousel with no pause button", "A looping background video with no stop control", "A news ticker or live score feed that cannot be paused"],
            check: ["Open each screen and wait. List everything that moves or changes without you touching it.", "For each item that runs longer than 5 seconds, find the visible pause, stop or hide control.", "Check that the control works by keyboard, not only by hovering over the moving area."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A homepage hero carousel auto-advances every 6 seconds and loops forever. The team says it pauses on hover. Is that enough?", options: ["No. Add a visible pause button that keyboard and touch users can reach", "Yes. Pausing on hover is a pause mechanism", "Yes, because each slide shows for longer than 5 seconds"], answer: 0, why: "Hover does nothing for keyboard, touch or screen reader users, so they still cannot stop it. The 5 seconds is about how long the motion lasts overall, not each slide."),
            spec: ["List every element that moves or updates automatically, with how long it runs.", "Draw the pause, stop or hide control for each, with its position, label and both states.", "Note whether the paused state is remembered when people move between pages."],
            review: ["What on this screen moves without anyone asking it to?", "Where is the pause button, and can you reach it without a mouse?", "Does this animation need to loop at all?"],
            tradeoff: "Marketing wants motion to catch the eye, and that is exactly what makes it hard for others to read. Let it play once and stop, or keep it running with a clear pause control near the start of the component.",
            system: "Give the carousel, video background and ticker components a built-in pause control that cannot be turned off.",
            pitch: "If people cannot stop the carousel, many of them stop reading the page instead."
        ),
        "2.3.1": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Photosensitive epilepsy", "Other seizure disorders", "Migraine", "Anyone who has not been diagnosed yet"],
            takeaways: ["Nothing on the page flashes more than three times in any one second, unless the flashes are below the general flash and red flash thresholds.", "Flashing can trigger seizures. The harm can be immediate and serious, so this is Level A.", "A flash is a quick change from light to dark and back. Saturated red flashing is treated more strictly.", "It covers everything on the page, including videos, ads and animations you did not make yourself."],
            failures: ["A strobing “breaking news” or sale banner", "A video with lightning, gunfire or camera flashes", "A rapid light-dark flicker used as an error or alert effect"],
            check: ["Count the flashes in each animation. More than three in any one second needs attention.", "Run videos and complex animations through a flash analysis tool before release.", "Check third-party content such as ads and embedded video, not only your own work."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A game’s win screen flashes the whole background between white and black five times a second for two seconds. What should you do?", options: ["Keep it, and add a warning before it plays", "Keep it, but let people turn it off in settings", "Change it to three flashes a second or fewer, or a non-flashing effect"], answer: 2, why: "A large, high-contrast flash at five per second is the exact pattern this rule forbids. A warning or an off switch does not help someone who has a seizure before they can react."),
            spec: ["Give the timing of every flashing or blinking effect, in flashes per second.", "Mark any video or animation that needs a flash analysis before it ships.", "Specify a slower or non-flashing version for any effect that came close."],
            review: ["Is there anything here that flickers or strobes?", "Who checks the videos and ads we did not design?", "Could this alert get attention without flashing at all?"],
            tradeoff: "Flashing grabs attention, which is why people reach for it. Use color, size or a single pulse instead. They get attention without the risk.",
            system: "Set motion tokens so no blink or pulse can run faster than three times a second, and forbid full-screen flashing in the guidelines.",
            pitch: "This is the one rule where getting it wrong can put someone in hospital. It costs us nothing to avoid."
        ),
        "2.3.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min · interactive", demo: .motion,
            who: ["Vestibular disorders", "Migraine", "Attention-related disabilities", "Motion sensitivity"],
            takeaways: ["Motion triggered by interaction — parallax, zooms, big slides — can cause dizziness, nausea and migraines.", "Respect the system “Reduce motion” setting. Developers read it as prefers-reduced-motion.", "Swap travel for fades or instant changes. Keep the feedback; lose the movement.", "It’s Level AAA, but because every OS exposes the setting, it’s one of the easiest AAA wins."],
            failures: ["Scroll-driven parallax with no off switch", "Full-screen zoom transitions between pages", "Backgrounds that shift as you scroll"],
            check: ["Turn on Reduce motion on your device and walk through the prototype.", "Spec a reduced alternative for every animation at handoff.", "Ask of each animation: is it essential, or decoration?"],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Your onboarding uses heavy parallax as people scroll. What does 2.3.3 ask for?", options: ["Remove all animation", "A way to turn that motion off", "Slow it below 3 per second"], answer: 1, why: "Most people can keep the motion — but those affected need to turn it off, for example by honoring Reduce motion."),
            spec: ["For every animation, specify a reduced version: a fade, or no transition.", "Mark which motion is essential to understanding, and why.", "Reference the system setting so developers wire up prefers-reduced-motion."],
            review: ["What does this flow look like with Reduce motion on?", "Which animation here is decoration, and which carries meaning?", "Does anything move without the person triggering it? That is 2.2.2."],
            tradeoff: "Motion is part of the brand. Reduced does not mean none: keep opacity and color changes, drop travel, scale and parallax.",
            system: "Give every motion token a reduced value, so each component gets the alternative for free.",
            pitch: "The setting already exists on every device and people already use it. Ignoring it means making some customers feel ill."
        ),
        "2.4.11": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Keyboard users", "Low vision with magnification", "Switch and voice control users", "Anyone on a small screen"],
            takeaways: ["When an element gets keyboard focus, it is not completely hidden by content you designed, such as sticky headers, footers, banners or chat widgets.", "If people cannot see what has focus, they cannot tell where they are or what Enter will do.", "At AA, part of the element may be covered. At AAA, 2.4.12 asks that no part of it is hidden.", "The fix is usually space: let the page scroll focused items clear of sticky bars."],
            failures: ["A sticky footer that hides fields as people tab down a form", "A cookie banner covering links at the bottom of the page", "A floating chat button sitting over the last item in a list"],
            check: ["Tab through every page with sticky headers, footers and banners showing.", "Watch for any moment when the focused element disappears behind them.", "Repeat at high zoom and on small screens, where sticky bars take more room."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Tabbing down a form, a focused button ends up half covered by an 80px sticky footer. Which is true?", options: ["It passes 2.4.11, but would fail 2.4.12 at AAA. Fix it anyway", "It fails 2.4.11 because part of it is covered", "It passes everything, because sticky footers are allowed"], answer: 0, why: "2.4.11 only fails when the element is entirely hidden. Half hidden is still hard to use, so leaving space for the footer is the better design."),
            spec: ["Give the height of every sticky header, footer and banner.", "Ask for scroll padding equal to those heights, so focused items stop clear of them.", "Say how banners and chat panels are dismissed, and whether they can be minimised."],
            review: ["What is sticky on this screen, and how much room does it take at 200% zoom?", "What happens when someone tabs to the last field on the page?", "Does this banner need to cover content at all?"],
            tradeoff: "Sticky bars keep actions in reach but eat into the visible area. Keep them short, let them collapse on small screens, and make sure the page scrolls focused items into the space that is left.",
            system: "Expose sticky header and footer heights as tokens that set the page’s scroll padding automatically.",
            pitch: "If the focused button is behind our footer, a keyboard user is pressing Enter on something they cannot see."
        ),
        "2.4.13": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Low vision", "Keyboard users", "Older adults", "Attention-related disabilities"],
            takeaways: ["A focus indicator is at least as large as a 2 CSS px thick outline around the unfocused component, and its pixels change by at least 3:1 between unfocused and focused states.", "It is Level AAA. At AA, 2.4.7 only asks that focus is visible.", "The 3:1 compares the same pixels before and after focus. 1.4.11 compares colors against what is next to them.", "A solid 2px outline in a color with 3:1 against the background meets it for most components."],
            failures: ["A 1px outline, too thin to meet the size", "A subtle tint change on the button background", "A thin underline under a large card"],
            check: ["Measure the ring: is it at least 2px thick all the way around?", "Compare the ring color with what those pixels were before focus. Is it 3:1 or more?", "Check the ring on every surface the component can sit on."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A button’s focus style is a 1px solid dark blue outline with 8:1 contrast against the white page. Does it meet 2.4.13?", options: ["Yes. The contrast is well above 3:1", "No. Its area is smaller than a 2px perimeter", "Yes. Any visible focus style passes"], answer: 1, why: "Both parts must pass: size and contrast. A 1px outline covers about half the area of a 2px one, so it is too small however strong the color."),
            spec: ["Give the focus ring’s thickness, offset and color for each component.", "List the contrast between ring and background on each surface color.", "For thin or unusual shapes, such as inline links, show how the indicator is drawn."],
            review: ["Is this ring 2px or more all the way around?", "What was under the ring before focus, and how much does it change?", "Are we aiming for AAA here, or is 2.4.7 the target?"],
            tradeoff: "A bold ring can feel heavy in a refined interface. A two-tone ring, a thin light line inside a dark one, meets the size and contrast on any background and can still be tuned to the brand.",
            system: "Define one focus ring token at 2px or more with a tested color, and apply it in every component.",
            pitch: "A thicker, stronger focus ring costs almost nothing and helps everyone who has ever lost track of where they are on a page."
        ),
        "2.4.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Keyboard users", "Screen reader users", "Switch control users", "Low vision with magnification"],
            takeaways: ["When people move through a page with the Tab key, focus goes in an order that keeps the meaning and lets them use it.", "Usually that follows the reading order. It does not have to be strictly left to right, as long as it makes sense.", "When a dialog or menu opens, focus moves into it. When it closes, focus goes back to what opened it.", "This is about interactive elements. The reading order of all content is 1.3.2."],
            failures: ["Focus jumping between columns at random", "A modal that opens while focus stays behind it", "Content inserted after the trigger that focus never reaches until the end of the page"],
            check: ["Tab through the prototype or build. Does the order make sense at every step?", "Open and close every dialog, menu and drawer. Where does focus go?", "On forms, check that focus follows the fields in the order people fill them in."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A “Filter” button opens a modal dialog. Where should focus go?", options: ["Into the dialog, then back to the Filter button when it closes", "It stays on the Filter button. People can tab to the dialog", "To the top of the page, so people can start again"], answer: 0, why: "Focus left behind the dialog means people are operating content they cannot see. Returning it to the trigger lets them carry on from where they were."),
            spec: ["Number the focus order on complex screens, forms and anything with columns.", "For each dialog, menu and drawer, say where focus starts and where it returns.", "Note where new content appears, so focus can reach it next."],
            review: ["Tab through this for me. Did anything surprise you?", "When this panel closes, where does the person end up?", "Does focus order match the order you would explain this screen in?"],
            tradeoff: "A layout can look best with the main action at the top right, while the focus order wants it last. Place it where it belongs in the task, or annotate the order so the build matches the intent.",
            system: "Build focus handling into the dialog, menu and drawer components, so focus moves in and returns automatically.",
            pitch: "When focus jumps around, keyboard users lose their place and give up on the task."
        ),
        "2.4.6": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Screen reader users", "Cognitive disabilities", "People who skim", "Screen magnifier users"],
            takeaways: ["Headings and labels describe what follows. People should know a section from its heading alone.", "Put the distinguishing words first: “Billing address”, not “Address for billing”.", "Avoid vague headings where clarity is needed: “Overview”, “More”, “Details”.", "This one is about quality. Whether headings exist in code is 1.3.1."],
            failures: ["Several sections all headed “Details”", "Buttons all labelled “Submit” for different actions", "Clever headings that hide what the section contains"],
            check: ["Read only the headings. Could you find what you need?", "Read each label out of context. Is it still clear?", "Look for identical labels that mean different things."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Two sections of a settings page are both headed “Options”. What does 2.4.6 ask for?", options: ["Different heading levels", "Headings that describe each section", "Removing one of the headings"], answer: 1, why: "A heading has to describe its topic. The same heading on different content describes neither."),
            spec: ["Write final heading and label copy in the design, not placeholder text.", "Flag labels that need a content review.", "Where a visible label is shortened, note the full name."],
            review: ["If I read only the headings, would I know what this page does?", "Which labels only make sense next to their neighbors?", "Who wrote this copy, and has anyone tested it?"],
            tradeoff: "Brand voice wants personality. Navigation wants clarity. Put the voice in body copy and keep headings and labels literal.",
            system: "Publish naming guidance with the components: verbs for buttons, nouns for headings.",
            pitch: "Clear headings are how everyone skims. This fix helps every single user."
        ),
        "2.4.7": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Keyboard users", "Switch and voice control users", "Low vision", "Power users"],
            takeaways: ["Every interactive element shows a visible indicator when it has keyboard focus.", "Design it. Do not leave it to the browser default, and never remove it.", "A good target, from 2.4.13: a 2px outline, offset from the element, with 3:1 contrast.", "It has to work on every background the component sits on."],
            failures: ["The outline removed in the stylesheet", "A focus style so faint nobody notices it", "Custom components with no focus state drawn"],
            check: ["Tab through the prototype or build. Can you always tell where you are?", "Check the focus style on light, dark and image backgrounds.", "Include a focus state in every component’s state list."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A designer removes the focus outline because mouse users see it after clicking. What is the best response?", options: ["Agree. It is visual noise", "Keep a designed style that shows for keyboard focus", "Replace it with a color change on hover"], answer: 1, why: "Developers can show the ring only for keyboard use. Hover does nothing for people who do not use a pointer."),
            spec: ["Draw the focus state for every interactive component.", "Give the ring’s width, offset and color as values.", "Show it on each surface color the component can sit on."],
            review: ["Where is the focus state for this component?", "Does the ring survive on the darkest and lightest backgrounds?", "Does a sticky bar ever cover the focused element? That is 2.4.11."],
            tradeoff: "A strong ring can clash with a refined style. A two-tone ring, light and dark together, stays visible everywhere and can be tuned to the brand.",
            system: "One focus token, applied by every component. Nobody should design a focus ring twice.",
            pitch: "Without visible focus, a keyboard user is operating your product blindfolded."
        ),
        "2.5.1": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Tremors and limited dexterity", "One-handed use", "Head pointer and mouth stick users", "Voice control users"],
            takeaways: ["Anything done with a multi-finger gesture or a path-based gesture also works with a single pointer and no path: a tap, a click or a double tap.", "Multipoint means two or more fingers, like pinch to zoom. Path-based means the route matters, like a swipe or a drawn shape, not just where it starts and ends.", "Give a simple control for each gesture: + and − buttons on a map, arrows on a carousel, a menu for swipe-to-delete.", "Exception: the gesture is essential, such as a signature or freehand drawing. Gestures run by the browser or operating system, not your design, are not covered."],
            failures: ["A map that only zooms by pinching", "A carousel that only moves by swiping", "Swipe-to-archive with no other way to archive"],
            check: ["List every gesture in the prototype, then find its tap or click equivalent.", "Try each task using only one finger and single taps.", "Check that the alternatives are visible, not hidden behind a hint."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A store locator map zooms with pinch. What does it need to meet 2.5.1?", options: ["Visible + and − buttons", "A hint that says “Pinch to zoom”", "Nothing. Pinch is a standard gesture"], answer: 0, why: "Pinch needs two fingers at once, which many people cannot do. A hint explains the gesture but does not replace it; single-tap buttons do."),
            spec: ["For each gesture, name its single-pointer alternative and where it lives.", "Mark which gestures are shortcuts and which control is the main path.", "Call out any gesture you treat as essential, and why."],
            review: ["What happens here if someone can only tap?", "Is this swipe the only way to do this?", "Is this a path-based gesture, or a simple drag? A drag where only the endpoints matter is 2.5.7."],
            tradeoff: "Gestures keep screens clean, and visible buttons add clutter. Keep the gesture as a shortcut, and put the alternative somewhere quieter, such as an overflow menu or small controls on the edge.",
            system: "Give gesture-driven components, like carousels, maps and swipeable rows, built-in button alternatives that cannot be turned off.",
            pitch: "A gesture is a shortcut, not a front door. If it is the only way in, some customers cannot get in."
        ),
        "2.5.2": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Tremors and limited dexterity", "Low vision users who miss targets", "Cognitive disabilities", "Anyone who taps the wrong thing"],
            takeaways: ["For single-pointer actions, pressing down does not complete the action. It completes on release.", "Then people can slide off the target before letting go to cancel. Or, if it fires, they can undo it.", "Press-and-hold that shows something, like a preview, is fine if letting go hides it again.", "Exception: acting on press is essential, such as playing a note on a piano app. This is mostly built by developers; you decide which actions are risky and how undo works."],
            failures: ["Buttons that fire the moment a finger touches them", "A delete that happens on press with no undo", "Drag and drop that cannot be cancelled once picked up"],
            check: ["In the build, press a button, slide off it and release. Did anything happen?", "List the destructive actions and confirm each has undo or a way to cancel.", "Check that drag and drop cancels when dropped outside a valid target."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Engineers want a “Delete” button to fire on touch-down so it feels faster. What do you recommend?", options: ["Fire on press. Speed matters most", "Fire on release, and cancel if the finger slides off", "Fire on press, with a confirmation dialog every time"], answer: 1, why: "Release is the moment people can still change their mind. Acting on press removes that chance, and a dialog on every delete adds friction for everyone."),
            spec: ["State that actions complete on release, not on press.", "Design the pressed state, so people see the action is about to happen.", "For risky actions, specify the undo, such as a toast with an Undo button."],
            review: ["What happens if someone touches this by mistake?", "Can this action be undone?", "Is there any reason this must fire on press?"],
            tradeoff: "Acting on press can feel snappier in games and music apps. Outside those cases, a clear pressed state gives the same feeling of speed while keeping the chance to back out.",
            system: "Base button and list-item components fire on release, and destructive actions use a standard undo toast.",
            pitch: "Everyone has tapped the wrong thing. Firing on release means a slip costs nothing."
        ),
        "2.5.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Voice control users", "Screen reader users who can see", "Cognitive disabilities", "People using speech input after injury"],
            takeaways: ["When a control has visible text, its accessible name contains that same text.", "Voice users say what they see, like “click Search”. If the hidden name is “Find products”, the command fails.", "Best practice: start the name with the visible text, then add context, like “Read more about pricing”.", "Icon-only buttons have no visible text, so this rule does not apply to them. They still need a name under 4.1.2."],
            failures: ["A “Buy” button named “Add to basket” in code", "Visible label “Next” with hidden name “Continue to payment”", "Extra hidden words added in front of the visible text"],
            check: ["For each control with visible text, compare it with the name you annotate.", "Say the visible label out loud as a voice command. Would it match?", "Check that added context comes after the visible words, not before."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Several cards each show a “Learn more” link. You want each link’s name to be unique. What do you annotate?", options: ["“Learn more about savings accounts”", "“Savings accounts details”", "“Open savings account page”"], answer: 0, why: "It contains the visible text and starts with it, so voice users can say what they see. The others drop the visible words."),
            spec: ["Annotate the accessible name for every control whose name differs from its visible text.", "Write the visible label as the first words of that name.", "Note where visible text is an image of text, so the name includes those words too."],
            review: ["What would I say to click this?", "Does the coded name match the words on screen?", "Why is the name different here?"],
            tradeoff: "Writers want short visible labels and full hidden names for context. Keep both by adding context after the visible words, or make the visible label clearer.",
            system: "In the button and link components, document that a hidden name must begin with the visible label.",
            pitch: "Voice users click by saying what they read. If the code disagrees with the screen, the button does not exist for them."
        ),
        "2.5.4": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Devices mounted on wheelchairs", "Tremors and involuntary movement", "People who cannot shake or tilt a device", "Anyone on a bumpy ride"],
            takeaways: ["If moving the device, such as shaking or tilting, or moving in front of a camera triggers an action, an on-screen control does the same thing.", "People can also turn the motion response off, so a tremor or a bus ride does not trigger it by accident.", "Exceptions: the motion is essential, like a step counter, or it works through an accessibility-supported interface.", "This is about motion as input. Animation that moves on screen is 2.3.3 and 2.2.2."],
            failures: ["Shake to undo with no undo button", "Tilt to scroll with no way to turn it off", "A “wave to continue” camera gesture as the only option"],
            check: ["List every feature that reacts to device or body motion.", "Find the button or control that does the same thing.", "Find the setting that turns the motion response off."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Your app adds shake to report a bug. What else does it need?", options: ["A “Report a bug” button, plus a setting to turn shake off", "A tutorial explaining the shake", "A firmer shake threshold to avoid accidents"], answer: 0, why: "Some people cannot shake a phone, and others shake it without meaning to. The button covers the first group and the off switch covers the second."),
            spec: ["For each motion feature, name the on-screen control that does the same thing.", "Specify where the setting to disable motion lives, and its default.", "Mark any motion you treat as essential, and why."],
            review: ["What happens if this phone is clamped to a wheelchair?", "Could this fire by accident in someone’s pocket or on a bus?", "Where is the switch to turn this off?"],
            tradeoff: "Motion features feel playful and save screen space. Keep them as extras, never the only route, and make turning them off one clear setting.",
            system: "Pair every motion trigger with a standard button and a shared “Motion controls” setting.",
            pitch: "Shake and tilt are delightful until your phone is bolted to a wheelchair. A button and an off switch keep the delight without locking anyone out."
        ),
        "2.5.7": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Tremors and limited dexterity", "Head pointer and eye tracking users", "Trackpad and stylus users", "People who cannot hold a press"],
            takeaways: ["Anything done by dragging can also be done with a single pointer and no drag: taps or clicks. New in WCAG 2.2, Level AA.", "It covers sortable lists, sliders, kanban cards, resizable panels and map panning.", "Keyboard support does not count here. The alternative must work with a pointer. Keyboard access is 2.1.1.", "Exceptions: dragging is essential, or the drag is set by the browser and you have not changed it."],
            failures: ["Reorder handles with no move up or down option", "A slider that only responds to dragging the thumb", "File upload that only accepts drag and drop"],
            check: ["For every draggable element, find the tap or click path that does the same job.", "Try each drag task with single clicks only, never holding the button down.", "Check that the alternative is visible or easy to find, not a hidden gesture."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A kanban board lets people drag cards between columns. The cards are also keyboard operable. What else does it need?", options: ["Nothing. Keyboard support covers it", "A “Drag here” hint on each column", "A “Move to” menu on each card"], answer: 2, why: "Keyboard support helps keyboard users, but someone using a head pointer or a shaky hand still cannot hold and drag. A move menu gives them a click-only path."),
            spec: ["For each drag interaction, specify the single-pointer alternative: buttons, a menu, or tap to pick up and tap to place.", "For sliders, state that tapping the track sets the value, or add step buttons.", "Note any drag you treat as essential, and why."],
            review: ["How would someone do this without ever holding the button down?", "Is the alternative findable, or only in a help article?", "Is this a drag or a path-based gesture like a swipe? Swipes are 2.5.1."],
            tradeoff: "Drag and drop feels direct and fast, and extra buttons add clutter. Keep the drag, and put the alternative in a menu on the item, so it is there when needed and quiet when not.",
            system: "Every draggable component ships with its non-drag alternative built in, such as a “Move” menu on sortable rows and step buttons on sliders.",
            pitch: "Dragging is one of the hardest things to do with a shaky hand or a head pointer. One menu item turns a blocked task into a finished one."
        ),
        "2.5.8": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min · interactive", demo: .taps,
            who: ["Tremors and limited dexterity", "Larger fingers", "People on the move", "Older adults"],
            takeaways: ["Targets need at least 24×24 CSS px. 44×44 — 2.5.5, Level AAA — is the comfortable goal.", "Spacing exception: a smaller target passes if a 24px circle centered on it doesn’t touch another target or another small target’s circle.", "Links inside sentences are exempt, as are targets whose size is set by the browser.", "The hit area can be bigger than the visible icon. Pad the glyph; don’t scale it."],
            failures: ["Dense toolbars of 16px icons touching each other", "A tiny × to close a modal", "Pagination and carousel dots"],
            check: ["Overlay a 24×24 square on every target in your file.", "For small targets, draw 24px circles at their centers — do any overlap?", "Annotate hit areas at handoff so developers build them."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A 20×20 icon button sits 20px away from the nearest other button. Does it pass?", options: ["No — it’s under 24px", "Yes — the spacing exception applies", "Only with a tooltip"], answer: 1, why: "A 24px circle centered on it doesn’t reach its neighbor, so the spacing exception applies. Still — bigger is kinder."),
            spec: ["Draw the hit area as its own annotated layer, with its size in px.", "Note the minimum spacing between neighboring targets.", "Call out anything under 24px and the exception it relies on."],
            review: ["Where is the smallest target on this screen, and who has to hit it?", "Are dense areas such as tables and toolbars leaning on the spacing exception?", "Is the hit area specified, or only the icon?"],
            tradeoff: "Density against reach. Keep the visual density if you must. Grow the invisible hit area and the spacing, not the glyphs.",
            system: "Set a minimum hit area on the base button and icon button: a 24px floor, 44px by default on touch.",
            pitch: "A missed tap is an error, a retry or an abandoned task. That is true for anyone on a moving train, not only people with tremors."
        ),
        "3.2.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and memory disabilities", "Screen reader users", "Low vision with magnification", "Anyone using the site often"],
            takeaways: ["Navigation that repeats across pages, such as a header, side menu or footer, appears in the same relative order each time, unless the user changes it.", "People learn where things are. When the order shifts, they have to find everything again, and some cannot.", "“Relative order” means items can be added or removed, as long as the shared items keep their order.", "Labeling the same function the same way each time is a different rule, 3.2.4."],
            failures: ["Main nav items reordered on different sections", "A footer whose links change order from page to page", "Search moving before the menu on some pages and after it on others"],
            check: ["Line up the headers and nav from every template side by side. Do shared items keep their order?", "Check the footer and side menus the same way.", "Check that any personalised order is something the user chose."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "In the account area, a “Billing” link is added to the side nav between existing links. Does this fail 3.2.3?", options: ["Yes. The nav must be identical on every page", "Yes, unless the new link is added at the end", "No. The shared links still keep their relative order"], answer: 2, why: "The rule is about relative order, not identical menus. Adding a link is fine as long as the links that repeat stay in the same order."),
            spec: ["Define the order of every repeated nav block once, in one place.", "Show which items may be added on certain sections, and where they go.", "Note any user setting that changes the order, so it is the only exception."],
            review: ["Is this nav built from the same component as on every other page?", "Why is the order different here?", "If someone uses this page every day, could they find the menu with their eyes closed?"],
            tradeoff: "Each team wants its own section promoted first in the nav. Keep the order fixed and show the current section with a selected state instead.",
            system: "Make headers, nav and footers shared components, so the order is defined once and cannot drift.",
            pitch: "People should not have to relearn our menu on every page. Consistent navigation is faster for everyone."
        ),
        "3.2.4": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and memory disabilities", "Screen reader users", "Voice control users", "Anyone learning a new product"],
            takeaways: ["Components that do the same thing across a set of pages are identified the same way: same label, same icon, same accessible name.", "Consistent does not mean identical text every time. “Go to page 4” and “Go to page 5” are consistent.", "Text alternatives count. A search icon is “Search” on every page, not “Find” on one and “Look up” on another.", "It is about naming. The order of repeated navigation is 3.2.3, and matching visible and coded labels is 2.5.3."],
            failures: ["“Save” on one screen and “Done” on another for the same action", "Two different icons for the same function", "An icon button with a different hidden label on each page"],
            check: ["List every repeated action and its label, icon and accessible name across screens.", "Look for the same function with different words or icons.", "Check icon-only buttons’ accessible names, not just their visuals."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Saving changes is “Save” in the editor, “Done” in settings, and a floppy disk icon in the profile. What is the fix?", options: ["None. Each page is consistent with itself", "Choose one label and icon and use it everywhere", "Add tooltips that explain each version"], answer: 1, why: "The function is the same, so its identity should be too. Tooltips explain the inconsistency without removing it."),
            spec: ["Keep a list of shared actions with their label, icon and accessible name.", "Annotate icon-only buttons with the exact accessible name.", "Flag any place where a familiar action is named differently on purpose, and why."],
            review: ["Is this the same action we call something else elsewhere?", "Does this icon mean the same thing on every screen?", "What will a screen reader say for this button here, and on the other pages?"],
            tradeoff: "Different teams and contexts pull toward local wording. Agree on one name per function, and allow small variations only when the function itself is different.",
            system: "Put shared actions in the component library with fixed labels, icons and accessible names, and keep a content glossary.",
            pitch: "If “Save” has three names, people have to learn our product three times. One name per action makes it easier for everyone."
        ),
        "3.3.1": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Screen reader users", "Color vision deficiency", "Cognitive disabilities", "Anyone filling in a long form"],
            takeaways: ["When the system detects an input error, it shows which field is wrong and describes the error in text.", "A red border or a warning icon alone is not enough. Some people cannot see the color, and nobody can read an icon’s meaning for sure.", "The message names the problem: “Enter your email address”, not just “Invalid”.", "This applies to errors the system catches. It does not force you to add validation, but whatever you detect must be explained."],
            failures: ["A field that only turns red", "A banner saying “There were errors” with no field named", "Errors shown only as an icon or a shake animation"],
            check: ["Submit the form empty and with wrong formats. Read every message with the colors turned off.", "Check that each message sits next to its field and says which field it is about.", "For long forms, check that a summary at the top lists each error and links to the field."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A sign-up form marks a bad email by turning the field border red. What do you add?", options: ["A text message by the field: “Enter an email address like name@example.com”", "A red warning icon inside the field", "A tooltip on the border that appears on hover"], answer: 0, why: "The error must be identified and described in text. An icon is still only a visual cue, and a hover tooltip hides the text from keyboard and touch users."),
            spec: ["Write the error text for every validation rule on every field.", "Show where each message appears and how it is linked to its field.", "Note when validation runs: on submit, or when leaving the field."],
            review: ["If I could not see red, how would I know which field is wrong?", "Does every error say what went wrong, in words?", "Where does focus go after a failed submit?"],
            tradeoff: "Inline errors that fire while people type feel helpful but can nag before they finish. Validate when they leave the field or submit, then show clear text that stays until the error is fixed.",
            system: "Give the field component an error state with a required text slot, so an error cannot be shown with color alone.",
            pitch: "A form that only says “something is wrong” is a form people abandon."
        ),
        "3.3.2": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and memory disabilities", "Screen reader users", "Low vision with zoom", "Anyone interrupted mid-form"],
            takeaways: ["Every input has a label that stays visible while people type.", "Placeholders are not labels. They vanish, and are often low contrast.", "Say what format you need before people get it wrong: date order, password rules.", "Mark required fields in words, or with a symbol you explain."],
            failures: ["Placeholder-only fields", "Format rules revealed only in the error", "A group of fields, such as a date, with one vague label"],
            check: ["Fill in the form, then look again. Can you still tell what each field is?", "Check that every hint appears before the input, not after an error.", "Check that required and optional differ by more than color."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A date field accepts only DD/MM/YYYY. Where does that instruction belong?", options: ["In the error message", "Visible with the label, before typing", "In the placeholder"], answer: 1, why: "Instructions prevent errors. A placeholder disappears at the moment it is needed."),
            spec: ["Write the label, hint and required state for every field.", "Note which hint text stays on screen.", "For grouped fields, give the group label and each part’s label."],
            review: ["Which fields lose their label once filled?", "What does someone need to know before typing here?", "Could the form ask for less?"],
            tradeoff: "Labels above fields cost vertical space. Floating labels save it, but must stay readable in size and contrast once floated.",
            system: "Make label a required property on the field component, with hint and required built in.",
            pitch: "Every unclear field is a support ticket or an abandoned form."
        ),
        "3.3.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and learning disabilities", "Screen reader users", "People with dyslexia", "Non-native speakers"],
            takeaways: ["When the system detects an error and knows how to fix it, it tells the person how.", "“Invalid date” describes. “Enter the date as DD/MM/YYYY, for example 07/03/2025” suggests.", "Offer the likely fix: a close match for a misspelled city, the allowed range for a number.", "Leave out a suggestion when it would weaken security or defeat the purpose, such as hinting at a password or a test answer."],
            failures: ["“Invalid input” with no hint of what is valid", "Password rules revealed one at a time, after each failure", "A list of accepted values that the system knows but does not show"],
            check: ["For each error message, ask: does it say what to do next?", "List every rule the system checks, and make sure its error names the rule.", "Check that no message gives away a password, security answer or test answer."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A login fails because the password is wrong. What should the error say?", options: ["“Your password should be the one with your dog’s name”", "“Your password starts with K”", "“That email and password do not match. Try again or reset your password”"], answer: 2, why: "Hinting at the password would jeopardize security, which is an exception. You can still point to a safe next step, such as resetting."),
            spec: ["Pair every error with its suggested fix, written out in the file.", "Note where the fix is known, such as format, range or a near match, and where it is not.", "Flag messages that must stay vague for security, and why."],
            review: ["Does this message tell me how to fix it, or only that I failed?", "What does the system already know that it is not telling me?", "Is anything here vague for a real security reason, or just by habit?"],
            tradeoff: "Specific messages take more writing and more states to design. Start with the most common errors from analytics or support tickets, and write those well first.",
            system: "Keep an error message library in the system, with each validation rule paired with its tested wording.",
            pitch: "Telling people how to fix an error is the cheapest way to get them past it."
        ),
        "3.3.4": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and learning disabilities", "Motor disabilities and tremors", "Screen reader users", "Anyone who misclicks"],
            takeaways: ["When a page makes a legal commitment or financial transaction, changes or deletes user data, or submits test answers, mistakes must be fixable.", "Do at least one: make the action reversible, check the input and let people correct errors, or show a review step to confirm before final submission.", "A review screen before “Pay” or “Sign”, an undo after “Delete”, or a cancellation period all count.", "Other forms are not covered by this AA rule, though the same patterns help everywhere."],
            failures: ["A one-tap purchase with no review and no cancel", "Delete with no confirm and no undo", "A test that submits answers the moment the last one is picked"],
            check: ["List every action in the flow that spends money, signs something, changes saved data or submits a test.", "For each, point to the screen or state that gives the second chance.", "Check the review step shows what will actually happen: amount, item, recipient."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A banking app sends a transfer as soon as people tap “Send”. What is the lightest fix that meets the rule?", options: ["A toast that says “Transfer sent”", "A review screen with the amount and payee, and a “Confirm” button", "A help article explaining how to call support"], answer: 1, why: "A chance to review and confirm before final submission meets the rule. A toast confirms after the fact and reverses nothing."),
            spec: ["Mark each covered action and which safeguard it uses: undo, check, or confirm.", "Design the review screen and the undo state, with how long undo stays available.", "Write what the confirm button says, so it names the action, like “Pay £40”."],
            review: ["If I tapped this by mistake, how would I get back?", "Does the review step show the details that matter?", "Which actions here change saved data, and are they covered?"],
            tradeoff: "Extra steps slow down frequent users. Undo often beats a confirm dialog: it is fast for the confident and safe for everyone else.",
            system: "Provide a review screen pattern and an undoable delete pattern, and point to them from every destructive or payment component.",
            pitch: "A second chance before money moves costs one screen. A mistaken payment costs a complaint, a refund and trust."
        ),
        "3.3.7": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and memory disabilities", "Motor disabilities", "People who use switches or voice", "Anyone on a small screen"],
            takeaways: ["In one process, do not make people type the same information twice. Fill it in for them, or let them pick it.", "The classic fix is a “Same as billing address” checkbox, or a list of addresses they already gave.", "It applies within the same process, such as one checkout or application, not across separate visits.", "Exceptions: when re-entering is essential, needed for security, or the earlier information is no longer valid."],
            failures: ["Shipping and billing addresses both typed in full", "An email asked for on step 1 and again on step 4", "A review step that clears fields when people go back to edit"],
            check: ["Map every field in the flow and look for the same information asked twice.", "Step backward and forward through the prototype. Does entered data survive?", "For each repeat, choose auto-fill or a selectable option, and draw it."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "A five-step application asks for the applicant’s phone number on step 1 and a contact number on step 4. What is the best design?", options: ["Ask again, so people can confirm it", "Remove the step 4 question entirely", "Pre-fill step 4 with the step 1 number, still editable"], answer: 2, why: "Auto-populating meets the rule and keeps the option to give a different number. Removing the question may lose a real need."),
            spec: ["Mark each field that reuses earlier data, and name its source step.", "Say whether it is pre-filled or offered as a choice, and that it stays editable.", "Note any repeat kept on purpose, and which exception it falls under."],
            review: ["Where in this flow does the person tell us something twice?", "What happens to their data when they press Back?", "Is this repeat really for security, or just how the form was built?"],
            tradeoff: "Pre-filled data can be wrong for this step, like a work address reused as a delivery address. Pre-fill or offer it, but keep it visible and editable.",
            system: "Build address and contact patterns with a “use a saved or earlier value” option built in.",
            pitch: "Asking for the same thing twice feels careless to every customer, and blocks some of them."
        ),
        "3.3.8": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Cognitive and memory disabilities", "People with dyslexia or dyscalculia", "Password manager users", "Anyone who forgets passwords"],
            takeaways: ["No step of logging in may rely on a cognitive function test, such as remembering a password, transcribing a code or solving a puzzle, unless something helps.", "Help can be another method that is not a test, such as a passkey or an email link, or support like pasting and password managers.", "A password field passes if people can paste into it and password managers can fill it.", "At AA, recognizing objects, or picking a photo the user provided, is allowed. 3.3.9 (AAA) removes that exception."],
            failures: ["Paste blocked in password or code fields", "A distorted-text CAPTCHA with no alternative", "One-time code fields that break when a code is pasted"],
            check: ["Walk every login, sign-up and verify step and list what each asks people to remember, copy or solve.", "Try a password manager and paste in every field of the flow.", "For each test you find, name the alternative or the assist that covers it."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Your security team wants to block paste in the password field to stop attacks. What do you decide?", options: ["Allow paste and password manager autofill", "Block paste, but add a “Show password” toggle", "Block paste, and add a security question as a backup"], answer: 0, why: "A password with no help is a memory test. Paste and password managers are the mechanism that assists, so blocking them fails the rule, and a security question is just another memory test."),
            spec: ["State that every credential and code field allows paste and autofill.", "Give password and code fields the right autocomplete tokens.", "List the other login methods offered, such as passkey or email link, and where they appear."],
            review: ["What does this login ask me to remember, copy or solve?", "Have you tried it with a password manager and paste?", "If a CAPTCHA is here, what is the alternative?"],
            tradeoff: "Security teams sometimes block paste or add puzzles to stop bots. Blocking paste mostly stops password managers, which makes passwords weaker. Use passkeys, rate limits and invisible bot checks instead.",
            system: "Make the code-entry component accept a pasted code across all its boxes, and never offer a paste-blocking option on password fields.",
            pitch: "Every locked-out user is a support call. Letting password managers work is safer and cheaper."
        ),
        "4.1.2": DeepLesson(
            meta: "5 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Screen reader users", "Voice control users", "Switch users", "People using custom assistive tools"],
            takeaways: ["Every control tells assistive technology what it is (role), what it is called (name) and what state it is in (value and states).", "When the state changes, such as expanded, checked or selected, assistive technology is told.", "Developers build this. Designers decide the name, every state, and which standard component a design really is.", "Native controls do this for free. A custom-drawn dropdown or toggle has to rebuild all of it."],
            failures: ["A clickable div that is not announced as a button", "An icon-only button with no name", "An accordion that never says if it is expanded"],
            check: ["For each custom component, name the standard control it behaves like: button, link, checkbox, tab, switch.", "List every state it can show, and check each one is drawn and named.", "Give every icon-only control a name in the file."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "Your design shows a “Filters” chip that opens a panel and shows a count when filters are on. What do you hand off?", options: ["The chip’s visual states only. Developers will work out the rest", "“Make it accessible” on the frame", "Role: button. Name: “Filters”. States: expanded or collapsed, and the active count in the name"], answer: 2, why: "Role, name and state are design decisions. Without them, developers guess, and screen readers hear a generic or misleading control."),
            spec: ["Write the role of each custom component, using standard names like button, switch or tab.", "Write the accessible name of every icon-only control.", "List the states to expose, such as expanded, selected, checked, pressed or disabled."],
            review: ["What is this control, really? A link, a button, a tab?", "What would a screen reader call it?", "Which states here exist only visually?"],
            tradeoff: "Custom components give brand control but cost accessibility work in every one. Style native or well-tested library components where you can, and build custom only when the experience truly needs it.",
            system: "Document each component’s role, name source and exposed states beside its visual spec, so every use inherits them.",
            pitch: "If assistive technology cannot tell what a control is, the control does not exist for its users."
        ),
        "4.1.3": DeepLesson(
            meta: "4 min read", demo: nil,
            who: ["Screen reader users", "Screen magnifier users", "Cognitive disabilities", "People who look away from the screen"],
            takeaways: ["Status messages are announced by assistive technology without moving focus to them.", "A status message reports a result, progress, a wait, or an error, without changing context: “Saved”, “12 results”, “Uploading”, “3 errors”.", "If focus is moved to the message, as with a dialog, it is not a status message under this rule.", "Developers code the announcement. Designers decide which messages exist, their exact words and how urgent each is."],
            failures: ["A toast that appears and fades with no announcement", "Search results updating with no count announced", "A cart badge that changes silently after “Add to cart”"],
            check: ["List every message that appears without a page change: toasts, banners, counts, spinners, inline results.", "For each, write the text a screen reader should say.", "Mark which ones can wait politely and which ones must interrupt."],
            quiz: Quiz(question: "After “Add to cart”, a toast says “Added” and the cart count goes up. Focus stays on the button. What do you annotate?", options: ["Move focus to the toast so it is read", "Announce the toast as a status message, such as “Added to cart. 3 items”", "Nothing. The cart count changing is enough"], answer: 1, why: "A status message should be announced without taking focus away from where the person is working. Moving focus interrupts them, and a silent change tells them nothing."),
            spec: ["Mark each status message, its announced text and whether it is polite or urgent.", "Note that focus stays where it is when the message appears.", "Say how long a toast stays, and where people can find it again."],
            review: ["How would someone who cannot see this know it happened?", "Does focus jump anywhere it should not?", "Is this message an update, or does it need a decision, which makes it a dialog?"],
            tradeoff: "Announcing every small change gets noisy. Announce results and errors, keep the wording short, and do not repeat what the person just did.",
            system: "Build toasts, inline alerts and live counts with announcement built in, and document the polite or urgent setting.",
            pitch: "A toast nobody hears is a feature that only works for people who can see it."
        ),
    ]

